<LegalLayout title="Privacy Policy - Walkable: Walks &amp; Places" description="Privacy Policy for the Walkable: Walks &amp; Places iOS app.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Walkable: Walks &amp; Places</p>
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="effective">Effective date: 5 November 2026</p>

  <p>Walkable (&ldquo;the app&rdquo;) is designed to respect your privacy. This policy explains, in plain terms, what the app does and does not do with information.</p>

  <h2>Summary</h2>
  <p>Walkable does not collect personal information about you or share it for advertising or tracking, and it has no account to create and no sign-up. The app does not use advertising, analytics, or tracking of any kind. Your walks, routes, and stats are stored only on your device.</p>

  <h2>Information we collect</h2>
  <p>We do not collect your name, email address or any account details, and we have no server. To show place facts and weather, the app sends location coordinates, without any identifier, to the third-party services listed below.</p>

  <h2>Location</h2>
  <p>Walkable asks for location access &ldquo;While Using the App&rdquo; to record the route of a walk. It never asks for or uses &ldquo;Always&rdquo; location access. Recording continues while your phone is locked once a walk has started, using this same permission together with the app&rsquo;s background location setting.</p>
  <p>Once a walk ends, Walkable looks up the places and facts to show on that walk&rsquo;s summary. To do this, it sends the coordinates of the finished route to the following public services:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Wikipedia and Wikidata, to find articles and facts about places near your route</li>
    <li>OpenStreetMap, through its public Overpass service, for named parks, reserves, and other mapped places</li>
    <li>the NSW Heritage Register, for heritage-listed places near your route (New South Wales only)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If your phone is offline when a walk ends, this is retried automatically the next time you open that walk&rsquo;s summary.</p>
  <p>Separately, while the app is open, Walkable sends your current location to Open-Meteo to show the weather strip and rain forecast. This is not tied to any particular walk.</p>
  <p>Only the coordinates needed for each request are sent. We do not send your name, your device identifier, your account details, or any other personal information with these requests, because the app has none to send. Your use of these services is subject to their own terms; we have no agreement with any of them and no access to anything they may keep on their own servers.</p>
  <p>Your route is also shown using Apple Maps, including the map on a walk&rsquo;s summary and the small map images in your walk history, and start and nearby suburb names are looked up using Apple&rsquo;s own on-device location service. These are part of the iPhone&rsquo;s operating system, not a service we operate.</p>

  <h2>Apple Health</h2>
  <p>If you choose to allow it, Walkable reads heart rate and active energy recorded by Apple Health for the time of a walk, to show them on that walk&rsquo;s summary. These values are read again from Apple Health each time you view a summary; the app does not keep its own copy of them.</p>
  <p>If you save a walk, Walkable can write it to Apple Health as a walking workout with its route, if you allow this. This is optional: a walk still saves in the app if you do not allow Apple Health access.</p>
  <p>Health data read by the app is never sent to us, to any third-party service named above, or to anyone else. It is used only to show it to you.</p>

  <h2>Motion and fitness</h2>
  <p>Walkable reads your step count from your iPhone&rsquo;s motion sensors to show steps and cadence for each walk, and to work out which parts of a walk were likely spent in a vehicle so they can be left out of your walking stats. This stays on your device.</p>

  <h2>Notifications</h2>
  <p>If you allow it, Walkable can show you a local notification if a walk is still recording with no movement for a while, as a reminder in case you forgot to stop it. This notification is scheduled and shown entirely on your phone; nothing about it is sent to us or to any server.</p>

  <h2>Information stored on your device</h2>
  <p>Everything the app keeps is stored only on your device, in its own local database: your walks, their routes, your stats, the facts shown for each walk, and your walk history. This information never leaves your phone, except through your own standard Apple iCloud device backup, which is controlled entirely by you and your Apple account; we have no access to it.</p>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>You can delete any saved walk from your walk history at any time, which removes that walk and its route from the app&rsquo;s local database. Deleting the app from your iPhone removes all of its data, including your walk history, unless it remains in your own iCloud device backup under your control.</p>

  <h2>Advertising, analytics, and tracking</h2>
  <p>Walkable contains no advertising, no analytics, and no tracking technologies. We do not build a profile of you and we do not follow you across apps or websites.</p>

  <h2>Selling or sharing data</h2>
  <p>We do not sell, rent, or share any personal information, because we do not collect any.</p>

  <h2>Children&rsquo;s privacy</h2>
  <p>The app does not collect personal information from anyone, including children.</p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>If this policy changes, we will update the effective date above and post the revised policy at this address.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>If you have any questions about this policy, contact <a href="mailto:support@mikhailnarbekov.com">support@mikhailnarbekov.com</a>.</p>
</LegalLayout>
